@@ -61,17 +62,21 @@ logging goes to stderr, so a test can parse stdout for the readiness line alone.
 Every startup failure prints a specific diagnostic to **stderr**, exits **non-zero** (`2`), and
 prints no readiness line and serves no request.
 
-| Condition | Exit | Diagnostic (shape) |
+| Condition | Exit | Diagnostic (actual text) |
 |---|---|---|
-| `serve` subcommand missing or unknown | `2` | `argparse` usage error |
+| `serve` subcommand missing or unknown | `2` | `argparse` usage error on stderr |
 | `--port` missing | `2` | `argparse`: `the following arguments are required: --port` |
 | `--data` missing | `2` | `argparse`: `the following arguments are required: --data` |
-| `--port` not an integer (`--port abc`) | `2` | names the invalid `--port` value |
-| `--port` out of range (`0`, `-1`, `65536`, `99999`) | `2` | names the valid range `1-65535` |
-| `--data` cannot be created (e.g. a parent is a file) | `2` | names the path and the OS reason |
-| `--data` exists but is not writable | `2` | names the path and that it is not writable |
-| `--data` exists but is not a directory | `2` | names the path |
-| `PORT` already in use | `2` | names the address and that it is in use |
+| `--port` not an integer (`--port abc`) | `2` | `pastebin: --port must be an integer between 1 and 65535, got abc` |
+| `--port` out of range (`0`, `-1`, `65536`, `99999`) | `2` | `pastebin: --port must be an integer between 1 and 65535, got <value>` |
+| `--data` cannot be created (e.g. a parent is a file) | `2` | `pastebin: cannot create storage directory <path>: <OS reason>` |
+| `--data` exists but is not writable | `2` | `pastebin: storage directory <path> is not writable: <OS reason>` |
+| `--data` exists but is not a directory | `2` | `pastebin: storage directory <path> exists but is not a directory` |
+| `PORT` already in use | `2` | `pastebin: 127.0.0.1:<port> is already in use` |
+
+Writability is established by creating and removing a file inside the directory, not with
+`os.access(W_OK)`, which is advisory and reports the wrong answer under containers and ACLs
+(R-013).
 
 Diagnostics for hand-detected failures are prefixed `pastebin: ` (R-013). No failure path
 returns `0`, and no failure path prints to stdout (Constitution II).
diff --git a/specs/001-pastebin-service/contracts/http-api.md b/specs/001-pastebin-service/contracts/http-api.md
index e712346..eafd565 100644
--- a/specs/001-pastebin-service/contracts/http-api.md
+++ b/specs/001-pastebin-service/contracts/http-api.md
@@ -28,6 +28,12 @@ format is not part of it (see [data-model.md](../data-model.md)).
 - A request body larger than **1,048,576 bytes** (`Content-Length`) is refused `413` before the
   body is read (FR-024, R-003).
 - Responses are HTTP/1.1 and always carry an explicit `Content-Length`.
+- A request refused *before* its body was read (the `413` above, a missing or unparsable
+  `Content-Length`, a `Transfer-Encoding` request) ends the connection: the unread body bytes
+  would otherwise be parsed as the next request line. The server writes the error response,
+  briefly discards whatever the client is still sending — in bounded chunks, never buffered —
+  and closes. The discard is what lets a client pushing a body larger than the socket buffers
+  still read the `413` instead of seeing a connection reset.
 
 ---
 
